# 2026-07-27 (v0.9.1): HOST-ONLY patch — the v0.9.0 byte-packing shipped a bundle-parse bug. PackedBytes
# serialises via serialize_bytes, which serde_json emits as a JSON array (a sequence), but the deserialiser
# accepted only a native bytes type — so every bridge bundle with a non-empty txs/tx_prevouts (every block
# with a real spend; block 170 is the first) failed to parse in prove-range-bridge. Fixed by also accepting
# a JSON sequence (host visit_seq). The GUEST is unchanged, so THIS ID IS UNCHANGED and every existing proof
# stays valid — v0.9.1 only rebuilds the host binaries + hardens the coordinator (release-on-failure,
# fail-fast errors, `hazync selftest` + `/api/meta`, bundle round-trip CI regression).
#
# 2026-07-31 (RUSTSEC-2026-0220): dependency-only re-baseline. `ruint` 1.19.0 -> 1.20.0, fixing
# "Uint shift operations: incorrect overflow flags and truncated shift amounts" (advisory published
# 2026-07-08, surfaced when the RustSec database updated). NO Hazync source changed — not one line of
# guest code — but ruint IS compiled for riscv32im (libruint-*.rlib sits under the guest target beside
# ark_bn254 / ark_ff / ark_groth16, reached through risc0's in-circuit assumption verification), so the
# ELF changes and the id moves with it. Measured rather than assumed: the same tree at ruint 1.19.0
# produced 85dc0b56, at 1.20.0 it produces this id.
#
# Taken deliberately and immediately. The board had 0 blocks proven at the time, so the cost was a few
# hours of rebuild; a week of contributed GPU time later it would have cost all of it. Bundles are
# NOT affected — leaf hashing is SHA-256 in the host and the guest C++, and touches no bignum
# arithmetic — so the witness corpus carries across unchanged. Validated: bundle round-trip PASS and
# the block-170 regression PASS with the tip matching, in the canonical fixed-path container.
# Superseded the earlier id 85dc0b56…
