# 2026-07-30 (domain separation): the accumulator now DOMAIN-SEPARATES leaf and interior hashes —
# leaf = SHA256(0x00 || preimage), interior = SHA256(0x01 || left || right). Previously both were bare
# SHA256 over their operands, and SECURITY.md carried this under "documented, not changed
# (non-exploitable, deliberately deferred to avoid churn/re-prove)". The "non-exploitable" half does
# not survive checking: a UTXO leaf preimage is 57 + |scriptPubKey| bytes, so a 7-byte scriptPubKey
# yields a 64-byte preimage — exactly what an interior node hashes — and the stated barrier (leaf
# preimages open with an uncontrollable txid) is a grinding cost, not a separation, because a txid is
# the hash of a transaction an attacker composes. The accumulator is the one non-Core component and the
# piece named for external audit, so it is the worst place to rest on an argument instead of a property.
# Changed in all three implementations that must agree byte for byte (host oracle, guest Rust Stump,
# and BOTH guest C++ leaf builders); scripts/check-utreexo.sh gates them. EVERY LEAF HASH CHANGES, so
# no prior proof is valid under this id and the board restarts from genesis — accepted deliberately,
# since the run was moving to resized hardware from genesis regardless. Validated: bundle round-trip
# PASS and block 170 regression PASS with the tip matching, in the canonical fixed-path container.
# Superseded the earlier id 3f52baff…
#
# ALSO FIXED IN THIS BUILD, and the reason the first attempt failed: prover/methods/guest/build.rs
# emitted NO cargo:rerun-if-changed directives, so the C++ half of the guest was invisible to cargo's
# fingerprint. The first rebuild relinked the ELF from a verify_input.o three days stale — the Rust
# tagged interior nodes while the C++ left leaves untagged — and METHOD_ID CHANGED ANYWAY, because the
# Rust did rebuild. A changed id proves something in the guest changed; it never proves everything did.
# Only the block-170 regression caught it. The C++ sources, Core's translation units and the coreshim
# are now declared dependencies.
#
# 2026-07-27 (v0.10.0): SPEED re-baseline — libsecp256k1's `ECMULT_WINDOW_SIZE` 15 -> 19. This is a pure
# compile-time space/time trade inside libsecp (bigger precomputed `pre_g` table -> fewer wNAF additions
# per scalar multiply); NO consensus code changed, and the EC algorithm, ECDSA logic and every line of
# Core stay exactly as they were. Measured optimum: -1.88% cycles on block 130000, -2.30% on 140000,
# -1.79% on 741000 (post-taproot, 670 inputs); 20 regresses to -1.65% as the table doubles (~16MB -> 32MB)
# and paging overtakes the saving. The earlier sweep that declared window 15 "already optimal" had only
# swept DOWNWARD (4..15) — it never tested above the default, because the checked-in precomputed_ecmult.c
# hard-errors there (`#if ECMULT_WINDOW_SIZE > 15 -> #error`). Windows above 15 therefore require the table
# to be REGENERATED, which build.rs now does with libsecp's OWN generator (src/precompute_ecmult.c). That
# output is deterministic EC maths — byte-identical on any machine — so the id stays reproducible; the
# regeneration is skipped when the on-disk table is already ours. Guest ELF changed -> new id. Validated:
# regress + full adversarial suite + negative tests + BIP30 + script-flags + retarget differential (476
# real-chain vectors) + chainparams anchor + bundle round-trip, and 130000/140000/741000 still VALID with
# identical tip hashes, cum_work and UTXO-leaf counts. Superseded the earlier id 68819a54…
